 \emph{Cross-vendor and constraint-type sensitivity.} Cross-LLM-family Pillar~3 places $\mainReplRatioTriple{}\times$ and $\mainReplVTwoRatioTriple{}\times$ catalogues from Qwen-397B and gpt-oss-120B within $\dualFamilyDelta{}\times$, and Track-A re-extraction across \posebVendorsConfirmed{} vendor families lands \posebInBandCount{}/\posebTotalModels{} cataloguers in the $[\posebRatioBandLow{}\times, \posebRatioBandHigh{}\times]$ band (App.~\ref{app:cross_vendor_cataloguer}). The constraint-type subset probe E$_S$ shows that removing the author-dependent deviation channel preserves \cwtFourTypeRetentionPct{}\% of matched-pair discrimination (BSR $\cwtOrigBSR{}{\to}\cwtFourTypeBSR{}$); removing source-grounded omission collapses BSR to $\cwtTypedBSR{}{\approx}0.5$. Discriminative power is source-grounded, not author-chosen (App.~\ref{app:phase1_sensitivity}).
 
-\paragraph{Typed-CwT robustness at scale.} Removing the \textsc{deviation} channel (the only violation type whose definition depends on per-CPG \texttt{allowed\_actions} authoring) reverses the variance ordering on the manual 706-scenario subset ($\eta^{2}_{\mathrm{eval}}{=}0.059$ vs.\ $\eta^{2}_{\mathrm{run}}{=}0.076$, $N{=}16{,}944$) but preserves it on the auto-expanded \phaseBEpisodes{}-episode corpus ($\cresFiveEtaSqTyped{}$ vs.\ $\cresFiveEtaRunTyped{}$, ratio $\cresFiveTypedRatio{}{\times}$; App.~\ref{app:typed_cwt_robustness}); the headline attribution survives at scale. Auto-expanded scenarios are by design lower-severity --- the engine generates compliance variants and Tier-S edge cases without inflating the catalogue-critical violation pool, so the loose-consensus critical fraction drops from 6.6\% (manual headline) to 3.9\% on Phase~B; the FA-direction signal (process-oblivious evaluators systematically over-credit) is unchanged at either scale.
